1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,190
Hi, students. In this video,

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:03,285
we're going to be talking about

3
00:00:03,285 --> 00:00:05,160
Linux File I/O and some of

4
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:08,955
the basics of how to use File
I/O in user space in Linux.

5
00:00:08,955 --> 00:00:11,580
We're going to introduce
Linux File I/O,

6
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:12,975
we're going to talk about

7
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:15,255
buffered I/O versus
system calls,

8
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:19,740
and we're going to deal with
the most basic operation

9
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:22,540
that you're going to do on
files, which is opening them.

10
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,730
In terms of how you're
interacting with the kernel,

11
00:00:26,730 --> 00:00:28,170
files are also
going to be opened

12
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:30,030
via what's called
file descriptors.

13
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:33,465
You often see this
abbreviated as FDs.

14
00:00:33,465 --> 00:00:36,180
An FD is really just
represented by an integer.

15
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:38,550
Both the kernel and user
space are ultimately

16
00:00:38,550 --> 00:00:40,455
referring to files based on

17
00:00:40,455 --> 00:00:43,080
an integer value that's
tracked in the kernel.

18
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:44,855
The kernel is going to maintain

19
00:00:44,855 --> 00:00:47,330
a per process list of
files in a file table.

20
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:48,920
Every process is going to have

21
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,570
its own custom list of files

22
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:53,515
based on which file it's opened.

23
00:00:53,515 --> 00:00:55,460
That list of files
is going to be

24
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:57,020
indexed by the file descriptor,

25
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:59,545
which again is just an integer.

26
00:00:59,545 --> 00:01:02,150
Every process in Linux
is going to have

27
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:03,590
at least three file descriptors

28
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:06,725
open when it is created that is.

29
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:08,840
The first is standard in

30
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:10,910
which is the standard
input and this

31
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:16,245
is by historical precedent,
file descriptor 0.

32
00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:18,530
This is your terminal
input device.

33
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:20,240
If you're using the terminal

34
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,545
to interact with your program,

35
00:01:21,545 --> 00:01:22,880
this is where you're
going to be typing

36
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,850
input in from the keyboard.

37
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:26,710
The second is standard out,

38
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:28,810
which is file descriptor 1.

39
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:30,655
This is the terminal display.

40
00:01:30,655 --> 00:01:33,415
If you're looking
at print f output,

41
00:01:33,415 --> 00:01:34,990
this is where your print
f output's going to

42
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:37,295
be going to file descriptor 1.

43
00:01:37,295 --> 00:01:40,550
Then the third is standard
error or file descriptor 2.

44
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:41,710
This is also going to be

45
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:43,660
redirected to the
terminal display,

46
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:47,995
but it allows you to
separate which print

47
00:01:47,995 --> 00:01:53,025
output goes to possibly
different targets.

48
00:01:53,025 --> 00:01:54,910
This is useful because if you're

49
00:01:54,910 --> 00:01:57,385
having an error occur
in your program,

50
00:01:57,385 --> 00:01:59,755
you can send that to
a different path than

51
00:01:59,755 --> 00:02:01,390
normal standard output and then

52
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:03,265
possibly capture
that separately.

53
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:06,360
The output file descriptor

54
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:10,290
2 is where the error
messages are typically sent.

55
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,920
If you've done any type of
POSIX programming before,

56
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,140
you're probably used to fopen,

57
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:22,555
fwrite, and fread.

58
00:02:22,555 --> 00:02:25,490
That's typically what
you'll use if you're just

59
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:26,660
writing a Hello World type

60
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:28,879
programming and
looking at examples.

61
00:02:28,879 --> 00:02:30,560
One of the things that
you'll notice when we're

62
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,915
talking about Linux
system programming,

63
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:34,760
is that we're using the ones

64
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,290
that don't have the f prefix.

65
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:39,385
We're using open read,
write and close.

66
00:02:39,385 --> 00:02:41,570
The reason we are using the ones

67
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:43,360
without the "f" prefix is that

68
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,395
the "f" prefix functions

69
00:02:45,395 --> 00:02:48,120
relate to buffered I/O
and not system calls.

70
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:49,560
Again in this class,

71
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:50,960
we're going to be
talking about is

72
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:54,625
the interaction between user
space and kernel space.

73
00:02:54,625 --> 00:02:57,770
F calls are one step above

74
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:00,140
the system call level interface

75
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:01,600
and interaction with the kernel.

76
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:02,870
There's another layer of

77
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:04,390
buffering that's
happening there.

78
00:03:04,390 --> 00:03:05,555
Those are useful.

79
00:03:05,555 --> 00:03:07,775
You would typically
use them in programs,

80
00:03:07,775 --> 00:03:11,670
but we're going to
talk about the open or

81
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:13,680
the non "f" prefix versions of

82
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,410
file I/O just because that's

83
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:18,240
where the interaction is
happening with the kernel.

84
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:21,650
The non "f" prefix are the ones

85
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:24,160
that are going to be
using system calls.

86
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:26,690
One other thing that you'll
notice when you look at

87
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:29,185
a call signature of
open versus fopen,

88
00:03:29,185 --> 00:03:32,285
fopen is going to return
a file pointer and

89
00:03:32,285 --> 00:03:34,580
the syscall version
of open is going to

90
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:37,325
return that file descriptor
that we mentioned.

91
00:03:37,325 --> 00:03:39,650
This is what's
actually happening in

92
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,840
terms of the kernel and
user space interaction,

93
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,360
is an integer tracking
and that gets

94
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:46,280
abstracted into a file pointer

95
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:48,190
when you're using buffered I/O.

96
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:50,840
One other way that you
can see the difference

97
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,320
for this using the man
pages that I've described,

98
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:54,620
if you remember
when we originally

99
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:56,225
talked about man pages,

100
00:03:56,225 --> 00:03:57,890
I talked about the fact
that you can look at

101
00:03:57,890 --> 00:03:59,960
the corner here and
see references to

102
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:02,945
the original Linux
programmer specification

103
00:04:02,945 --> 00:04:05,060
from back in the 1970s.

104
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:06,920
One thing that you'll
notice when you look at

105
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:09,260
these two different man pages

106
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:10,580
is the fopen is

107
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,170
actually in a different
section of the manual.

108
00:04:14,870 --> 00:04:17,940
Because it's not a part of
this syscall interface,

109
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:19,670
it's actually in section
3 of the manual,

110
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:21,020
whereas the open functions are

111
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:22,400
in section 2 of the manual.

112
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:23,660
Again, just an indication

113
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:25,610
that we're dealing with a
different layer when we're

114
00:04:25,610 --> 00:04:27,470
looking at the fopen or

115
00:04:27,470 --> 00:04:31,640
the "f" prefix
version of file I/O.

116
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:34,760
If you dig into the
source code for glibc,

117
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:36,140
you can trace it
down to the point

118
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:37,550
that you can convince yourself

119
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:41,180
that fopen calls actually

120
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:43,940
are working with open
calls under the hood.

121
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:47,000
Here's an example of
following the path through

122
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,880
from where fopen is actually

123
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:52,144
defined and used in glibc

124
00:04:52,144 --> 00:04:54,860
and where it's
actually calling the

125
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:57,275
under open command that maps to

126
00:04:57,275 --> 00:05:00,575
a system call under the hood.

127
00:05:00,575 --> 00:05:03,770
We can verify that
fopen is really

128
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:07,530
just a wrapper on open that's
going to add buffering.

129
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:09,590
Again, the difference between

130
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:11,060
the fopen and open is just that

131
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:13,400
the "f" prefix functions
from the standard I/O are

132
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,920
going to provide the platform
independent user buffering.

133
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:19,865
There's more details on this in

134
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:21,590
Linux system
programming Chapter 3,

135
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:25,260
but ultimately file buffering

136
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:26,390
just means that rather than

137
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:27,800
reading and writing
a full block,

138
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,960
you're going to be reading and
writing copies and memory,

139
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,350
and that can improve
performance.

140
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:34,400
But what do we
specifically mean when we

141
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:36,660
say userspace file buffering?

142
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:39,545
We mean that in the
f's function cases,

143
00:05:39,545 --> 00:05:41,240
that buffering is
actually happening in

144
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:43,885
the userspace rather
than in the kernel.

145
00:05:43,885 --> 00:05:46,280
One benefit there is
that you can reduce

146
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:48,550
the amount of system calls
made into the kernel.

147
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:50,000
There's more details on this

148
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,760
again in Linux system
programming Chapter 3,

149
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:55,510
if you want to probe into
this in a little more detail.

150
00:05:55,510 --> 00:05:58,320
It's fine to use the f

151
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:01,590
based functions in your
programming assignments.

152
00:06:01,590 --> 00:06:02,910
Again, we're talking about

153
00:06:02,910 --> 00:06:05,690
the non "f" prefix one just
because we want to talk about

154
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:07,550
the details of the userspace and

155
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:10,740
kernel interaction for the
purpose of this course.

156
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:14,900
The open function
again maps really

157
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:16,520
close to what you're used to

158
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:18,485
if you've already used fopen.

159
00:06:18,485 --> 00:06:23,320
We're just matching a path
name to a file descriptor.

160
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:25,220
When we pass in open to

161
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:27,365
a path name of a file
that we want to open,

162
00:06:27,365 --> 00:06:29,870
we're going to be returned
a file descriptor we can

163
00:06:29,870 --> 00:06:32,695
use to reference that file
from within our process.

164
00:06:32,695 --> 00:06:37,160
The flags that we can include
are related to access,

165
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:39,890
whether we want
read-only, read-write.

166
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:41,990
If we want to create append.

167
00:06:41,990 --> 00:06:43,670
There's a bunch of
other things we can do

168
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:45,110
like synchronous
I/O that we'll talk

169
00:06:45,110 --> 00:06:49,090
about in later videos.

170
00:06:49,090 --> 00:06:51,350
Then when you are
creating the file,

171
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:52,610
there's a mode
argument that you can

172
00:06:52,610 --> 00:06:54,470
use that gives read, write,

173
00:06:54,470 --> 00:06:56,810
or execute permissions for
a user group or other,

174
00:06:56,810 --> 00:06:58,575
as we've already discussed

175
00:06:58,575 --> 00:07:01,930
using the Linux
permission scheme.